All three subclasses contain nothing except a constructor and handleBoundary(). Is a class this small worth having?,No - three near-empty classes are worse than one class with a mode flag,"Yes: the smallness is the evidence that the split worked. Each holds exactly the one thing that differs, and no variant can accidentally change any other part of a move",No - they should be merged into an enum of boundary behaviours,"Yes, but only because Java requires a class per file",,2,"A mode flag would put the three behaviours back inside one method and let a future edit there affect all three modes at once. That said, a boundary-handler passed in as a parameter would be a defensible alternative worth discussing.",2

Real code:

public class Board {
    public boolean isWithinBounds(int row, int col) {
        return row >= 0 && row < height && col >= 0 && col < width;
    }
}

public class ClassicSnakeGame extends SnakeGame {

    @Override
    protected Point handleBoundary(Point rawNewHead) {
        boolean withinBounds = getBoard().isWithinBounds(rawNewHead.getRow(), rawNewHead.getCol());
        return withinBounds ? rawNewHead : null;   // null -> hitting the wall ends the game
    }
}

ClassicSnakeGame asks the Board whether a position is in bounds rather than comparing against width and height itself. What does that buy?,Nothing - the comparison is short enough to inline,"The rule for what counts as on the board is stated once. CylinderSnakeGame does its own row comparison instead, which is exactly the duplication this avoids elsewhere",It makes the Board immutable,It allows the board size to change mid-game,,2,"Note the honest inconsistency worth pointing out in class: Cylinder open-codes 'row < 0 || row >= getBoard().getHeight()' rather than reusing a Board method, so the two variants now express the same idea differently.",2

